                    File Viewer v1.3.3 for The Major BBS v10
                     Copyright (c) 2026 Elwynor Technologies
              Originally (c) 1995 High Velocity Software, Inc.

 WHAT IS FILE VIEWER?

 File Viewer shows text files to your callers: plain text, ANSI art and
 IBM PC (code page 437) art.  Files are kept in a library of named
 documents.  Callers pick a document from the list, or go straight to one
 from a menu link, and read it page by page or as one continuous feed.
 The viewer can also slow its output to the speed of an old modem.

 INSTALLATION

 1. With the BBS down, copy GALFV.DLL, GALFV.MDF, GALFV.MSG and GALFV.RLN
    into your BBS folder.
 2. Add File Viewer to a menu with the Menu Tree editor (see MENU LINKS).
 3. Start the BBS.  File Viewer creates GALFVDOC.DAT (the library),
    GALFVSET.DAT (its settings) and a GALFV folder for documents.

 Upgrading from ELWFV (version 1.1): with the BBS down, delete ELWFV.DLL,
 ELWFV.MDF, ELWFV.MSG and ELWFV.MCV, install the GALFV files, and point
 your menu pages at File Viewer again.  No activation code is needed.

 THE LIBRARY

 Entering File Viewer from a menu page with no command string shows the
 library: a numbered list of the documents the caller may see.  Type a
 number or a name to view one, Enter for the next page of the list, L to
 list from the top, or X to leave.

 Callers holding the "add documents" key also see:
   A  add a document        E  change one        D  delete one
 and sysops also see:
   F  view any file on the server by its full path
   S  the settings

 MENU LINKS

 A module page in the Menu Tree editor has a command string.  Put a
 document's name there and the page opens that document directly; when
 the caller leaves it, they are back at the menu.  Callers may also type a
 name after the menu selection, for example "V RULES".

 If a link names a document that does not exist yet, a caller who may add
 documents is offered the chance to create it on the spot - so you can
 lay out your menus first and fill in the documents as you go.

 A sysop may also put a full server path in the command string; it is
 shown only to holders of the sysop key, and only while "Sysops may view
 any server file" is YES.

 VIEWING

 Page by page, the prompt under each page offers:
   N or Enter  next page          P  previous page      T  first page
   a number    go to that page    C  continuous from this page on
   K           keyword search     B  show or change the simulated baud rate
   X           leave
 Enter on the last page leaves the document.

 Keyword search takes up to six words ("K dragon sword" works too), then
 asks for the direction.  A page containing any of the words, as a whole
 word in any case, is shown; Enter then finds the next one.

 Continuous display sends the whole document without pausing.  Press
 Enter at any time to stop it.

 While a page is being sent at a simulated speed, Enter sends the rest of
 that page at once and X leaves.

 CONTENT TYPES

   TEXT  Word-wrapped to the caller's screen.  Escape codes are removed.
   ANSI  Shown as drawn, not wrapped.  ANSI callers see the colours and
         graphics; callers without ANSI see the text, with line drawing
         shown as + - | = and #.
   IBM   IBM PC character art: shown as drawn, with the same plain ASCII
         substitutes for callers without ANSI.
   AUTO  Decided from the file: .ANS, or ANSI escape codes inside, means
         ANSI; .ASC, .NFO, .DIZ or line-drawing characters mean IBM;
         anything else is TEXT.

 A Ctrl-Z ends a file (so the SAUCE description on ANSI art is not
 shown) and a form feed starts a new page.  Pages fit the caller's screen
 size from their account settings.  Very complex ANSI animation that
 moves the cursor around is best set to CONTINUOUS.

 ADDING DOCUMENTS

 Choose A at the library (or accept the offer from a menu link), give the
 document a name (2 to 15 letters, digits, - or _) and a title, and say
 where the content comes from:

   U  Upload a file with any protocol your board offers.  It is stored in
      the document folder as NAME.EXT (TXT, ANS, ASC, NFO, DIZ, IBM, DOC,
      LOG or MD; anything else is stored as .TXT).
   W  Write it now in the BBS editor (up to about 8,000 characters).
   L  (Sysops) link a file that is already on the server, anywhere.

 You then see the document's properties.  Type a number to change one:

   1  Title
   2  Key needed to view it (a single - lets anyone view it)
   3  Display mode: DEFAULT (the setting), PAGED, CONTINUOUS or ASK
   4  Content type: AUTO, TEXT, ANSI or IBM
   5  Simulated baud rate: DEFAULT, FULL, or a rate from 110 to 115200
   6  Hidden from the list: YES documents open only by name or link
   7  Continuous messages: SHOW or HIDE the "(Continuous display - press
      Enter to stop)" header and the "Stopped." line
   8  When finished: WAIT shows "End of ... Press Enter" after a
      continuous display; RETURN goes straight back to where the caller
      started - the menu, for a menu link
   9  Header: NONE, or the name of a header to show above the document
      (see HEADERS)
  10  File (sysops only): link the document to a server file instead

 With HIDE and RETURN, a menu link to a CONTINUOUS document shows the
 caller nothing but the file and then puts them back at the menu - good
 for ANSI screens and bulletins.

 or U to upload new content, W to edit the text online, V to view the
 document, X when done.  Callers with the add key may change and delete
 only the documents they added; sysops may change all of them.  Deleting
 a document also deletes its uploaded or written file, never a linked one.

 HEADERS

 A header is a file shown at the top of a document: a logo, a banner, a
 title bar.  One header can serve any number of documents.  Choose H at
 the library to see the headers, then A to add one (named and supplied
 just like a document: upload, write or link), a header's name to change
 it, or D to delete one.  A header's properties are its title, content
 type (AUTO, TEXT, ANSI or IBM), height and file.  To use a header, set a
 document's Header property to the header's name.

 For callers with ANSI, the header is drawn once and stays put: the
 document scrolls and pages in the rows beneath it.  Callers without ANSI
 see the header at the top of every page (and once at the start of a
 continuous display).  If a header is so tall that fewer than 5 lines of
 the document would fit under it on the caller's screen, it is shown once
 at the start and then scrolls away with the document.

 The height is counted from the file.  ANSI art that moves the cursor
 around cannot be counted, so give such a header its height in lines by
 hand (Height in lines, 1 to 99; AUTO counts again).  Documents without a
 header scroll as before.  Deleting a header leaves the documents that
 named it without one.

 Headers and documents share names: a header cannot have the name of a
 document, and the other way around.  Callers with the add key can add
 headers and change their own; sysops can change all of them.

 SIMULATED BAUD RATE

 Output can be held to the speed of a modem: a rate of 2400 sends about
 240 characters a second, as a real 2400 baud connection would.  Any rate
 from 110 to 115200 may be used (300, 1200, 2400, 9600, 14400, 28800 ...).
 The setting gives the default, each document can choose its own, and a
 caller can change it while reading with B and a rate (B 1200), or turn it
 off with B OFF.

 SETTINGS

 Settings are changed online only (there are no configuration editor
 options for File Viewer).  Choose S at the library; each change is saved
 and used at once, and noted in the Audit Trail.

   1  Key for sysop functions        default SYSOP
   2  Key to add documents           default SYSOP (blank = everyone)
   3  Folder for uploaded documents  default GALFV (in the BBS folder)
   4  Default display mode           default PAGED
   5  Largest upload, in KB          default 1024
   6  Default simulated baud rate    default OFF
   7  Sysops may view any server file  default YES

 R resets every setting to its default.

 FILES

   GALFV.DLL, .MDF, .MSG, .RLN   the module
   GALFVDOC.DAT                  the document library
   GALFVSET.DAT                  the settings
   GALFV\                        uploaded and written documents

 MESSAGES

 Every message callers see is in GALFV.MSG and can be changed with the
 configuration editor.  Changing values (a name, a page number) are
 named text variables such as GALFV_DOC and GALFV_PAGE, so they can be
 moved or removed freely.

 MODULE HISTORY

 File Viewer was written in February 1995 by Wizard Software.  It was
 acquired, along with the rest of GraphicWares and Wizard Software, by
 Wilderland Development, and Elwynor Technologies acquired Wilderland.
 Elwynor Technologies ported it to Worldgroup 3.2 in March 2006 (version
 1.1).  Version 1.2.0 is the conversion for The Major BBS v10, with the
 document library, menu links, uploads, online writing, continuous
 display and the simulated baud rate added.  Version 1.3.0 adds headers.

